<article class="post">
<header class="post-header wrap-narrow">
  <span class="eyebrow">Correctness · Transactions</span>
  <h1><%= post["title"] %></h1>
  <p class="post-meta"><time datetime="<%= post["date"] %>"><%= post["display_date"] %></time> · <%= post["read_minutes"] %> min read</p>
  <p class="post-lede">A reader asked whether SoliDB is ACID. This is the long answer, and every claim in it comes with the test behind it. A SoliDB transaction commits as one write, fsynced before it is acknowledged at every isolation level but one. Killed with <code>SIGKILL</code> 100 times while 32 clients moved money, SoliDB lost no acknowledged transfer and left none half applied. Concurrent transfers conserve money, since 2.9.1: 2.9.0 lost writes on a statement nearly every application writes, and the test that found it is now in the repository. Since 2.9.3 a transfer is one request, and PostgreSQL still commits 1.8 times as many at one client; among ten hot accounts SoliDB commits 1.6 times as many. Its isolation is weaker than its level names suggest, and two things are not tested yet: a power loss, and the anomalies only a cycle search finds.</p>
  <p class="post-note">Measured on November 9 and 10, 2026, on one machine (AMD Ryzen 9 9950X, NVMe RAID1, Rocky Linux 10), each database in a container limited to 4 cores and 16&nbsp;GB, with the harnesses in <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb/tree/main/benchmarks/compare"><code>benchmarks/compare</code></a> (<code>bank.sh</code>, <code>run.sh</code>) and <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb/tree/main/faults"><code>faults</code></a>, on the published images of SoliDB 2.9.0 to 2.9.3 and PostgreSQL 18.6. SoliDB ran on solistore. Throughput figures are the median of three 20-second runs. The raw results are committed beside the harnesses. The first half of this story is in <a href="/blog/solidb-against-postgresql-mongodb-arangodb-surrealdb#transactions">the comparison post</a>; this one goes further, and corrects one thing it said.</p>
  <p class="post-note"><strong>Updated on November 11 for 2.9.4</strong>, released the next day: locks wait, the two upper isolation levels check what was read, and a transfer costs a third less. PostgreSQL is now 1.22 times ahead at one client and 1.14 at 32, and SoliDB is ahead over five round trips at 32 clients. The text below is as published; the new measurements are in <a href="#v294">their own section</a>.</p>
</header>

<div class="post-body wrap-narrow">
