<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, function (1 request)</pre></td><td><pre><strong>21,674</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>44 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>113,920</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>556 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>10,406</pre></td><td><pre><strong>34 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>24 / 54 µs</strong></pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, run_transaction (1 request)</pre></td><td><pre>11,945</pre></td><td><pre>68 µs</pre></td><td><pre>68,903</pre></td><td><pre>953 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>16,204</strong></pre></td><td><pre>42 ms</pre></td><td><pre>80 / 98 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, READ COMMITTED (5)</pre></td><td><pre>10,555</pre></td><td><pre>92 µs</pre></td><td><pre>67,383</pre></td><td><pre>1.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre>8,339</pre></td><td><pre>44 ms</pre></td><td><pre>41 / 87 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, SERIALIZABLE (6)</pre></td><td><pre>8,361</pre></td><td><pre>118 µs</pre></td><td><pre>46,138</pre></td><td><pre>1.6 ms</pre></td><td><pre>7,663</pre></td><td><pre>109 ms</pre></td><td><pre>56 / 138 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, driver (5)</pre></td><td><pre>7,036</pre></td><td><pre>152 µs</pre></td><td><pre>49,283</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>10,160</pre></td><td><pre>70 ms</pre></td><td><pre>125 / 121 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, HTTP (5)</pre></td><td><pre>4,133</pre></td><td><pre>235 µs</pre></td><td><pre>20,135</pre></td><td><pre>3.0 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5,303</pre></td><td><pre>101 ms</pre></td><td><pre>197 / 327 µs</pre></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>One request each, PostgreSQL commits 1.8 times as many transfers as SoliDB at one client and 1.65 times as many at 32, with lower latency at the median and at the 99th percentile. Among 10 hot accounts SoliDB commits 1.56 times as many, though PostgreSQL&rsquo;s tail there is the shorter one (34&nbsp;ms against 42). What separates them is the cost of a transaction itself, once the round trips are gone: at one client SoliDB spends 80&nbsp;µs of database CPU on a transfer, PostgreSQL 24. Five requests each, SoliDB&rsquo;s driver is still behind PostgreSQL&rsquo;s <code>READ COMMITTED</code> without contention, and ahead of it among hot accounts.</p>
<p>The order of the runs matters, so here it is. PostgreSQL&rsquo;s function and SoliDB&rsquo;s <code>run_transaction</code> each ran alone on a fresh server, as did PostgreSQL&rsquo;s <code>READ COMMITTED</code> and SoliDB&rsquo;s HTTP mode; <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> and SoliDB&rsquo;s driver mode each followed one of those on the same server. On a server that had just run the HTTP and driver modes, <code>run_transaction</code> committed 10,374 transfers a second at one client (p50 109&nbsp;µs) instead of 11,945; at 32 clients and among hot accounts it made no difference. We have not established why.</p>
<p><strong>What 2.9.3 changed.</strong> Over the driver, 2.9.2 committed 38,518 transfers a second at 32 clients; 2.9.3 commits 49,283. Three changes did it. A staged update stays a value from staging to commit, where it used to be turned into a document and back three times, timestamps parsed and formatted along the way. solistore 0.3.2 wakes each committing writer on its own, hands the lead of the next fsync to the next writer in line, and lets the members of a group being fsynced sleep instead of spinning: at 32 fsynced writers, half the CPU per write. And the commit, which waits for its fsync, no longer holds up the other connections of its worker thread. One series went the other way: over the driver at one client, 2.9.3 commits 7,036 a second, against 7,563 for 2.9.2 (p50 152&nbsp;µs against 128). We have not found why yet.</p>
</section>

<section id="writes"><h2>Fsynced writes outside a transaction</h2>
<p>One document per request, an insert or an update of one field by key, each fsynced before it is acknowledged: PostgreSQL with its default <code>synchronous_commit=on</code>, SoliDB 2.9.3 with <code>SOLIDB_DURABILITY=full</code>. Both measured the same morning, after loading the same million documents, with <a href="https://github.com/solisoft/solidb/blob/main/benchmarks/compare/run.sh"><code>run.sh</code></a>:</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Fsynced</th><th>c=1 ops/s</th><th>c=1 p50</th><th>c=1 p99</th><th>c=32 ops/s (3 runs)</th><th>c=32 p50</th><th>c=32 p99</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, insert</pre></td><td><pre><strong>23,320</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>39 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>52 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>86,890 (86,040–88,772)</pre></td><td><pre><strong>279 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>546 µs</strong></pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL 18.6, insert</pre></td><td><pre>19,114</pre></td><td><pre>49 µs</pre></td><td><pre>74 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>88,307</strong> (76,449–102,017)</pre></td><td><pre>281 µs</pre></td><td><pre>2.0 ms</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, update</pre></td><td><pre><strong>22,901</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>40 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>53 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>88,243</strong> (87,809–88,541)</pre></td><td><pre><strong>321 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>597 µs</strong></pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL 18.6, update</pre></td><td><pre>14,823</pre></td><td><pre>59 µs</pre></td><td><pre>112 µs</pre></td><td><pre>49,506 (48,504–49,898)</pre></td><td><pre>404 µs</pre></td><td><pre>5.6 ms</pre></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>At one client SoliDB fsyncs an insert in 39&nbsp;µs and PostgreSQL in 49. At 32 clients the inserts are level: PostgreSQL&rsquo;s median run is 1.6% ahead, but its three runs spread from 76,449 to 102,017 a second as the table grows, while SoliDB&rsquo;s stay within 3%, with a 99th percentile a quarter of PostgreSQL&rsquo;s. Updates at 32 clients: 88,243 a second against 49,506.</p>
<p>The comparison post had SoliDB 2.9.0 at 81,298 inserts a second there, the one series it clearly lost. Part of that gap was the run, not the version. Run alternately the same morning, one 20-second run each, 2.9.0 inserted 86,964 and 87,448 a second and 2.9.3 92,058 and 89,906; updates, 80,981 and 83,141 against 88,703 and 87,665. solistore 0.3.2 is worth about 5% of throughput here, and, measured on its own, half the CPU per fsynced write.</p>
</section>

<section id="v294"><h2>Update: 2.9.4, the next day</h2>
<p>Three items of the list below were worked on at once, and 2.9.4 shipped on November 11. The numbers above are left as they were measured; these are the same benchmark, the same machine and the same protocol on the published 2.9.4 image, beside the PostgreSQL figures of November 9 and 10.</p>
<div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Every commit fsynced</th><th>1 client</th><th>p50</th><th>32 clients</th><th>p99</th><th>10 hot accounts, 32 clients</th><th>p99</th><th>DB CPU per transfer, 1 / 32 clients</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, function (1 request)</pre></td><td><pre><strong>21,674</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>44 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>113,920</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>556 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>10,406</pre></td><td><pre>34 ms</pre></td><td><pre><strong>24 / 54 µs</strong></pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, run_transaction (1)</pre></td><td><pre>11,945</pre></td><td><pre>68 µs</pre></td><td><pre>68,903</pre></td><td><pre>953 µs</pre></td><td><pre>16,204</pre></td><td><pre>42 ms</pre></td><td><pre>80 / 98 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.4, run_transaction (1)</pre></td><td><pre>17,728</pre></td><td><pre>53 µs</pre></td><td><pre>99,963</pre></td><td><pre>885 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>33,474</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>4.6 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre>56 / 55 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>PostgreSQL, READ COMMITTED (5)</pre></td><td><pre><strong>10,555</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>92 µs</strong></pre></td><td><pre>67,383</pre></td><td><pre>1.4 ms</pre></td><td><pre>8,339</pre></td><td><pre>44 ms</pre></td><td><pre><strong>41</strong> / 87 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, driver (5)</pre></td><td><pre>7,036</pre></td><td><pre>152 µs</pre></td><td><pre>49,283</pre></td><td><pre>1.3 ms</pre></td><td><pre>10,160</pre></td><td><pre>70 ms</pre></td><td><pre>125 / 121 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.4, driver (5)</pre></td><td><pre>9,720</pre></td><td><pre>99 µs</pre></td><td><pre><strong>71,889</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>1.0 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>19,814</strong></pre></td><td><pre><strong>7.6 ms</strong></pre></td><td><pre>72 / <strong>73 µs</strong></pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.3, HTTP (5)</pre></td><td><pre>4,133</pre></td><td><pre>235 µs</pre></td><td><pre>20,135</pre></td><td><pre>3.0 ms</pre></td><td><pre>5,303</pre></td><td><pre>101 ms</pre></td><td><pre>197 / 327 µs</pre></td></tr>
<tr><td><pre>SoliDB 2.9.4, HTTP (5)</pre></td><td><pre>4,404</pre></td><td><pre>221 µs</pre></td><td><pre>23,337</pre></td><td><pre>2.9 ms</pre></td><td><pre>9,179</pre></td><td><pre>16 ms</pre></td><td><pre>192 / 291 µs</pre></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>One request each, PostgreSQL still commits 1.22 times as many transfers at one client (it was 1.8) and 1.14 times as many at 32 (it was 1.65). Over five requests SoliDB&rsquo;s driver is now ahead of PostgreSQL&rsquo;s <code>READ COMMITTED</code> at 32 clients, 71,889 against 67,383, and still behind at one, 9,720 against 10,555. Among 10 hot accounts SoliDB commits 3.2 times as many in one request and 2.4 times as many in five, and its 99th percentile went from 42 to 4.6&nbsp;ms. At 32 clients a transfer now costs 55&nbsp;µs of database CPU, against PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 54; at one client 56 against 24.</p>
<p>What changed:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Locks wait.</strong> A document lock another transaction holds is waited for, 500&nbsp;ms at most (<code>SOLIDB_TX_LOCK_WAIT_MS</code>), and a deadlock is refused at once. At <code>read_committed</code>, an <code>UPDATE</code> whose document changed while it waited is evaluated again on the current document. Among hot accounts no transfer is refused any more, where 1.2 were for each one that committed. The harness now writes a transfer&rsquo;s two accounts in account order, as the PostgreSQL one always did: two transactions that take contended documents in opposite orders deadlock, on SoliDB as elsewhere.</li>
<li><strong>The commit costs less.</strong> On a quiet server it runs on the thread that received it instead of being handed to another, which was 14 of a transfer&rsquo;s 64&nbsp;µs. Under load a connection prepares its commit and hands it to one thread, which writes every commit waiting at that moment as one batch with one fsync, so no connection keeps a thread asleep through an fsync. solistore 0.4.0 inserts a small group&rsquo;s writes once, by the thread that fsynced them.</li>
<li><strong><code>repeatable_read</code> and <code>serializable</code> are no longer only names.</strong> What a transaction&rsquo;s queries read is recorded and checked at commit: a document read by key must still be at the revision read, and a collection read by a scan or an index must not have been written since. Otherwise the commit is refused with a <code>409</code>. The two doctors <a href="#isolation">above</a> can no longer both leave at <code>serializable</code>. Elle (Jepsen&rsquo;s checker) found the histories of both levels serializable on our append workload, on a build of <code>main</code> the day before the release, where 2.9.0 showed thousands of anomalies. <code>read_committed</code>, the default, still allows write skew, as it does everywhere.</li>
</ul>
<p>What it cost, and what is still true. The 99th percentile at 32 clients is not better than 2.9.3&rsquo;s by much (885&nbsp;µs against 953 in one request) and is longer than PostgreSQL&rsquo;s 556. The server now looks at its sockets every 4 tasks instead of every 61, which grouped commits need; point reads at 32 clients gained 13% from it and the 99th percentile of index reads at 32 clients went from 240 to 350&nbsp;µs. The order of the runs still matters, the other way round: after the HTTP and driver modes on the same server, <code>run_transaction</code> committed 110,540 a second at 32 clients instead of 99,963 on a fresh one. The kills were repeated on 2.9.4, over HTTP as above and over the driver, where the killed commits are grouped ones: 120 <code>kill -9</code> in all, 15.8 million acknowledged transfers, none lost and none half applied (<code>results/2026-11-11-v2.9.4/</code>). A power loss is still untested.</p>
</section>

<section id="behind"><h2>Where SoliDB is behind</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The cost of a transaction.</strong> With one request per transfer each, PostgreSQL commits 1.8 times as many at one client and 1.65 times as many at 32. At one client SoliDB spends 80&nbsp;µs of database CPU on a transfer, PostgreSQL 24; at 32, 98 against 54. <em>2.9.4: 1.22 and 1.14 times; 56 against 24&nbsp;µs at one client, 55 against 54 at 32 (<a href="#v294">above</a>).</em></li>
<li><strong>Locks that do not wait.</strong> A lock held by another transaction is refused at once. Among 10 hot accounts that is 1.2 refused attempts for every transfer that commits, each one a round trip the client pays for, and a 99th percentile of 42&nbsp;ms. PostgreSQL&rsquo;s locks wait, and its function commits without a single retry. Waiting locks, with deadlock detection, are on our list. <em>Done in 2.9.4: no retry, and a 99th percentile of 4.6&nbsp;ms.</em></li>
<li><strong>Isolation.</strong> No level is serializable, and the levels above <code>read_committed</code> are only names. Write skew through queries commits (<a href="#isolation">above</a>). Elle has not searched our histories yet. <em>2.9.4 checks what a transaction read at <code>repeatable_read</code> and <code>serializable</code>, and Elle found both serializable on our workload; <code>read_committed</code> is unchanged.</em></li>
<li><strong>Power loss is not tested.</strong> Every crash on this page is a process kill. Dropping what was written but not fsynced, below the filesystem (with LazyFS or dm-log-writes), is the next test in the <code>faults</code> harness.</li>
<li><strong>One node.</strong> A transaction does not span shards or nodes. Earlier versions had endpoints for distributed transactions that never applied a write; they were removed.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="run"><h2>Run it yourself</h2>
<p>Linux with Docker or rootful podman (<code>DOCKER="sudo podman"</code>). The CPU sets assume 16 cores; set <code>DB_CPUS</code> and <code>CLIENT_CPUS</code> for your machine.</p>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> terminal</div><pre>git clone https://github.com/solisoft/solidb && cd solidb/benchmarks/compare
cargo build --release --bin solidb-bank
./bank.sh postgres solidb-2.9.3                     <span class="tok-com"># throughput and the money checks</span>
ONLY_MODES=function ./bank.sh postgres              <span class="tok-com"># PostgreSQL's one-request transfer</span>
SOLIDB_CRASH=ghcr.io/solisoft/solidb:v2.9.3 \
  ./bank.sh crash-postgres crash-postgres-async crash-solidb crash-solidb-unsynced
python3 bank_report.py ~/bench/results/&lt;date&gt;/bank
./run.sh solidb-solistore-durable postgres-durable  <span class="tok-com"># fsynced inserts and updates</span>

cd ../../faults && cargo build --release            <span class="tok-com"># kill -9 on both engines, isolation histories</span>
target/release/solidb-faults crash --bin /path/to/solidb --engine solistore --durability full --cycles 20</pre></div>
<p>If a setting is unfair to PostgreSQL, or a check would miss something, open an issue or a pull request against the harness: we will rerun it and update this page.</p>
</section>
</div>
